WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:07.780
Proposition thirty-nine would add identification requirements each time Californians

2
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:08.120
vote.

3
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.520
Citizenship is already required for state and federal elections.

4
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.380
The question is how identity and eligibility are checked,

5
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.780
not whether those elections should allow noncitizens to vote.

6
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.920
Today, registration uses identifying information.

7
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.460
Most in-person voters sign a roster; mail voters sign

8
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.300
their ballot envelope, and election officials check that signature.

9
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.380
The proposal would add government-identification checks before ballots are

10
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:38.780
counted.

11
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.980
Imagine a voter preparing a mail ballot at home.

12
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.660
Under the proposal, that voter would select a government-issued

13
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.620
identification in their registration, then write its last four

14
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.380
digits on the ballot envelope.

15
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.240
Officials would compare those digits with the registration record.

16
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.540
An election worker helps explain the identification process.

17
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.960
An in-person voter would instead present government-issued identification.

18
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.400
The state must offer a free voter identification card

19
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.120
on request.

20
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.660
The Legislature would still have to clarify details, including

21
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.120
which forms of identification qualify.

22
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.400
Officials would make best efforts using government data to

23
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.520
verify citizenship on voter rolls, publish annual verification percentages,

24
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.640
and face state compliance audits every other year.

25
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.440
Those checks are separate from presenting identification at the

26
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.220
polling place.

27
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.260
The analyst estimates annual state and local costs from

28
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.940
tens of millions to the low hundreds of millions

29
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:41.800
of dollars.

30
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:47.140
Card demand, implementation rules, staffing, and technology choices affect

31
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:47.740
the total.

32
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.180
A free card still costs government money to provide.

33
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.980
This is a proposed change, not instructions for casting

34
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.640
your current ballot.

35
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.800
For voting today, use the official election instructions and

36
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.600
your county elections office.

37
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.600
A voter might favor a consistent identification check as

38
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.200
another verification layer and a way to strengthen confidence.

39
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.580
The free-card provision addresses the purchase cost of identification.

40
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.400
Whether the additional checks deliver benefits proportionate to their

41
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.160
costs is the question to weigh.

42
00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:25.000
You might ask what happens to eligible voters without

43
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:25.580
documents.

44
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:30.340
A skeptical voter could worry about administrative mistakes, obtaining

45
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.200
documents, or navigating new rules.

46
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.780
A free card does not remove every time or

47
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:37.960
paperwork burden.

48
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.960
Existing checks already operate, so opponents question the extra

49
00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.640
system’s necessity and its effect on access.

50
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.380
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 43

51
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:52.540
percent said yes, 55 percent no, and 1 percent

52
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:53.160
were unsure.

53
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.380
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

54
00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:01.940
A later September Berkeley survey found 39 percent yes,

55
00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.040
52 percent no, and 9 percent undecided.

56
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.220
Question wording and methods differ, so comparing polls does

57
00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.360
not by itself establish a trend.

58
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.460
The Independent Voter Project survey ending October second found

59
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:19.800
43 percent yes and 40 percent no.

60
00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.240
Its methods and wording differ again.

61
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.220
The disagreement is a reason for caution, not a

62
00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:27.720
reason to declare a winner.

63
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.040
Yes adds the proposed requirements; no retains the current

64
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.480
identification framework.

65
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.940
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

66
00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.580
and official voter guide.

67
00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:43.520
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
